* sweep: S dummies should be moved top-to-bottom to check whether it can be placed between the two nodes. Otherwise there is always the funny loop over the top. Overall, just implement global sifting (at least for S dummies, but maybe also for data edges, not sure).
* Gateway edges which are recognized as back edges are temporarily swapped, i.e. they *do not* go through the reverse node corner dummy transformation. This is done because they should be positioned next to the gateway (subject to regular rules in the dummy node placement logic). So from the right gateway, the edge going left, should actually be stored as an *incoming* edge with *is_reversed* set to true. For the left gateway, the incoming edge from the right side should temporarily be stored in the *outcoming* edges. However, during the port assignment phase, when bend dummies are added, this is ultimately restored, and then the bend dummy is actually converted into a back edge corner dummy.
* Unify back edge corner dummies and bend dummies. They should be the same (though depends on whether they go left or right) - gateways have their bend dummies created in a later phase compared to back edge nodes. Also, do a write-up (paper-like) for how this works, I will forget it myself at some point.
* The edges which go straight up from a gateway need to be marked `is_vertical` even for those back edge dummy node cases. Right now they are not treated as vertical and thus get those funny wonks (but must check whether there *really* is no other obstacle in the way, gotta think which phase is the correct to determine that).
* The gateway VerticalCollapsed should not happen on the branching side. On the non-branching side, where there is only one same-lane edge (regardless of those leaving the lane), it should just always go out straight to the side.

* PE-BPMN tee-external-root-access in a tee-pool should not name itself (@tee-pool)
* PE-BPMN tee-software-operators should have a similar `on-premises` placeholder as tee-hardware-operators. `local`? as `on-premises` seems weird. (ChatGPT recommends replacing existing 'on-premises' with 'local' as well, not sure if I agree, must sleep over that suggestion)
* is `on-premises` for hardware-access doing the wrong thing?
* pe0001 end events are not drawn as end events (the message event)
* PE-BPMN the selectors (e.g. secure-channel begin and end) should better filter their node types. Right now one can accidentally select data nodes which doesn't make sense and results in a non-helpful assertion error.
* error in pe bpmd tool should result in error of test suite.
* D1.1, page 53 figure 12 subprocess P5, is a good diagram that should be layoutable now.
* Split the binary into a crate and a binary.
* Document infallability of the crate: The crate is meant to be used with catch_unwind: `assert`s are sprinkled throughout the codebase, `.unwrap()`s and index accesses are used everywhere (they are kind-of like assumptions). But the logic is complicated and I am 100% there are remaining hidden logic bugs. (well, right now it is just a binary, so one gets failure isolation "for free", but if I ever make this into a crate, then `catch_unwind`)
* Also, make it that boundary event nodes are more likely to leave downwards. Maybe have at all just a general node ID ordering?
* with boundary events, have their XY edge length constraint smaller? See `overview-boundary-events.bpmd`, should have nice straight regular outgoing sequence flow.
* fix the doc/test/build.sh to use Make/parallel (or Rust threadpools - or `fd` which also has a jobqueue) so we can have bounded parallelism.
* Also handle JXI routing: swap up-down order to down-up order (or vice versa), or if it is unavoidable then move JXIs the the IXI handling. (JXI is an edge cross where only one side (top or bottom) has the same y coordinate)
* Add more checks for the correctness of the BPMN semantics:
  * Check that the gateway branching and joining nodes match (use a dedicated graph analysis phase, not in the parser)
* Saw some youtuber who talks about generating BPMNs via ChatGPT. I guess I should write him when the tool is in a more usable state, then he can evaluate that: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=StqnUeWv6Lc
* Check whether t0033.bpmd can be fixed or is too weird a situation.
* Add `Edge::is_detached`: For DF edges which will be directly routed (TODO direct routing check should include that it does not add intersections with other edges of the same node). They should ideally only leave or enter at the edges, as this will be the least interruptive? But not sure about this.
* pe-bpmn MPC should be able to be in multiple pools, or at least in multiple lanes. And then specify multiple admins.
* pe-bpmn data_already_protected is not used anywhere, yet.
* pe-bpmn TEEs could be in a cyclic dependency of being each other's admin. This is currently reported as an error but in principle is solvable.
* Use a more performance hasher for all the hash maps.
* docsite, secure channel prot and unprot data associations are quite funky. Maybe the ad-hoc solution is to tackle the problem of distributing data objects better (or, at all) across layers?
* Expand the secure-channel shorthand
* Parse config values from the .bpmd file (see common/config.rs which is already macro-ified for this purpose).
* Try to help the crossing reduction phase by solving some variables, if the order can be answered for some nodes trivially (just one node in a given pool/lane/layer coordinate). Some larger diagrams with mostly trivial ordering have rather high ILP solving times. The solver is not very optimized, hence we better help it.
* Support data associations for Gateways.
* data elements have wonky y-coordinate differences, although it looks like they should all be at the same height. Also, they are oftentimes not put into the next halflayer, even though there is enough room.


Something from chatgpt 5.5, being mostly focused on the parser side:

>>>>>>
    Gateway IDs are parsed but never registered.
    to_gateway_outer keeps ids in GatewayNodeMeta, but parse_gateway never calls node_id_matcher.register, unlike events/activities/data. This means @gateway_id will not work for message flows, layout constraints, PE-BPMD refs, etc.

    Data continuation can panic on first data statement.
    In parse_data, meta.is_continuation uses SdeId(self.graph.data_elements.len() - 1). If the file starts with & ..., or continuation appears before any data element, this underflows/panics instead of returning a parse error.

    Duplicate explicit node IDs are silently accepted.
    NodeIdMatcher::register just pushes metadata, and find_node_id returns the first exact ID match. Two nodes with the same @id produce deterministic but wrong/ambiguous behavior. I would reject duplicates at registration time.

    Fuzzy node matching can silently pick the wrong node.
    If exact ID matching fails, find_node_id returns the best fuzzy match without a threshold or ambiguity check. For correctness-sensitive PE-BPMD/layout/data references, that is risky: a typo can bind to an unintended node.

    Reachable todo!() panics in PE-BPMD analysis.
    compute_accessible_data has several user-input-reachable todo!() branches for nested/disjoint protection graph comparisons. These should become ParseErrors before this branch is considered robust.

    The parser intentionally allows unfinished lifelines at EOF.
    There is an error block for active lifelines at EOF, but it is commented out. If this is not deliberate syntax, inputs missing a terminal event will parse as valid partial graphs.
<<<<<<

